# NOBORU JAPANESE LEARNING RULE

Version: 1.0

Priority: CRITICAL

Status: AUTHORITATIVE

This rule governs Japanese-specific learning presentation, pedagogy, types, and learner-facing language UX within Noboru feature modules.

Every component and type involved in lessons, reviews, readings, listening activities, kanji study, grammar explanation, and vocabulary drills must follow this document, docs/jlpt-content-architecture.md, and docs/NOBORU LEARNING ARCHITECTURE BIBLE.md.

Noboru teaches Japanese with respect for the language and the learner.

⸻

PURPOSE

The purpose of this rule is to ensure:

* Correct, learner-appropriate presentation of Japanese text
* JLPT-aligned progression and labeling
* Furigana and reading support where pedagogy requires it
* Respectful, accurate linguistic representation
* Reading and listening activities designed for real acquisition
* Types and UI that encode language structure explicitly

Language learning UX is part of the product system.

Not decorative localization.

⸻

REQUIREMENTS

Pedagogy

* Follow the educational model in docs/jlpt-content-architecture.md:
  Exposure → Understanding → Recognition → Recall → Production → Mastery
* Follow the runtime learning flow in docs/NOBORU LEARNING ARCHITECTURE BIBLE.md:
  Discover → Practice → Reinforce → Apply → Test → Master → Review Forever
* Activities must target a clear skill: recognition, recall, production, reading, or listening
* Difficulty must match declared JLPT level and learner stage
* Avoid punishing learners for respectful alternative valid answers when domain rules allow variants

JLPT Levels

* N5 through N1 labeling must be consistent across features
* Level indicators must reflect authoritative progression data, not UI-only badges
* Do not imply official JLPT certification where Noboru provides preparation only

Furigana and Readings

* Support furigana for learner-appropriate kanji presentation
* Furigana display must be controllable by learner settings where product supports it
* Store and transport readings separately from surface text in types where applicable
* Do not destroy reading information by over-normalizing Japanese strings in UI

Kanji

* Present stroke order, radicals, readings, and meanings with clear hierarchy
* Distinguish on-yomi and kun-yomi when relevant to the lesson goal
* Avoid overloading one screen with every reading at once unless review context requires it

Grammar

* Explain patterns with natural example sentences sourced from content systems
* Highlight structure without reducing grammar to vague glosses
* Use respectful, natural Japanese in examples

Vocabulary

* Show word type, pitch accent metadata when available, and context examples when appropriate
* Distinguish transitive and intransitive pairs, suru verbs, and common collocations when pedagogically relevant

Reading

* Support graded reading with appropriate furigana policy per level
* Line breaking and ruby text must remain readable on mobile
* Reading questions must test comprehension, not trick wording

Listening

* Listening prompts must remain accessible with transcripts or support modes where product rules allow
* Audio controls must be keyboard accessible
* Do not rely on color alone for playback state

Respect

* Use accurate Japanese, not machine-sounding or misleading shorthand in learner-facing content
* Avoid stereotype-driven copy or cultural mockery
* Present politeness levels accurately when teaching grammar or speech patterns

Types

* Feature types must model language data explicitly:
  surface form, reading, meaning, level, part of speech, audio URL, example sentences, furigana policy
* Avoid flattening Japanese entries into plain strings when structure matters

⸻

CODING STANDARDS

Components

* Use dedicated renderers for ruby text and furigana instead of ad hoc HTML strings
* Separate display components from session logic
* Keep answer checking in services, not JSX

Types

* Define VocabularyEntry, KanjiEntry, GrammarPoint, ReadingPassage, ListeningItem types in feature types/
* Prefer explicit optional fields over ambiguous any

Settings Integration

* Respect learner preferences for furigana, romaji display if supported, and audio autoplay policy

Accessibility

* Ensure furigana and main text remain usable with screen readers where feasible
* Provide textual alternatives when animated stroke teaching is used

Content Usage

* All learner-facing Japanese content comes from content systems per content.mdc
* Components format and present; they do not author curriculum

⸻

SUCCESS CRITERIA

Japanese learning work is successful when:

* Japanese text renders correctly on mobile with readable ruby text
* JLPT labeling is consistent and honest
* Furigana behavior matches learner settings and level policy
* Reading and listening activities support real comprehension
* Types encode linguistic structure clearly
* Pedagogy aligns with docs/jlpt-content-architecture.md
* Presentation remains respectful and accurate

⸻

FAILURE CRITERIA

Japanese learning quality is degraded when any of the following occur:

* Furigana baked incorrectly into immutable strings with no reading metadata
* JLPT levels used as cosmetic badges unrelated to content difficulty
* Romaji substituted silently where kana literacy is the goal
* Reading screens with illegible ruby layout on small devices
* Listening activities with no accessible control or support path
* Grammar explanations that are vague or misleading
* Answer checking in components with incomplete Japanese normalization rules
* Cultural stereotype examples or careless translations in learner UI

When failure criteria appear, fix language modeling and presentation before adding new activities.

⸻

EXAMPLES

Correct type

VocabularyEntry includes word, reading, meanings[], jlptLevel, partOfSpeech, exampleSentences[].

Correct furigana rendering

RubyText component receives structured tokens, not a single unbroken HTML string from content authors.

Correct reading screen

Passage view applies furigana policy from learner settings and level rules.

Incorrect modeling

type Word = string for all vocabulary everywhere.

Incorrect pedagogy

N5 screen showing dense literary passages without support.

Incorrect checking

Component compares raw strings only and rejects valid kanji variant answers without service rules.

⸻

ANTI-PATTERNS

Plain-string Japanese everywhere

Hand-built ruby markup scattered across features

JLPT labels with no connection to content metadata

Overuse of romaji in kana-learning stages

Illegible furigana sizes on mobile

Listening UI with invisible state and no keyboard control

Grammar tooltips that restate English only with no pattern structure

Hardcoded example sentences in components

Mixing politeness levels in one explanation without clarity

Using anime tropes as substitute for pedagogical examples

